\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Main Stock Index Rises 2.23% To End At Record   \b0\par\par\uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2007\par 22:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's main stock indexes climbed to new record highs Tuesday, for the ninth consecutive day, lifted by strength in copper prices and by gains on Wall Street.   \par \par \uc2 According to preliminary results from the Lima Stock Exchange, the stock market's broad general index rose 391.20 points, or 2.23%, to end at 17,911.83.   \par \par \uc2 The selective blue-chip index increased 847.10 points, or 2.88%, to finish at 30,291.16.   \par \par \uc2 "This market has been gaining for nine sessions and keeps hitting new records. Today's market was boosted by rising metals prices. Investors focused on copper miners in particular given the strong rise in copper prices," said one trader.   \par \par \uc2 Copper miner Sociedad Minera Cerro Verde SAA (CVERDEC1.VL) and  \uc2 Southern Copper Corp\uc2  (PCU) continued to catch investor interest over the course of the session.   \par \par \uc2 Southern Copper rose 3.5% to end at $75.30, while Cerro Verde gained 2.6% to end at $21.85.   \par \par \uc2 Various other blue chip shares also climbed.   \par \par \uc2 Compania de Minas Buenaventura\uc2  SAA (BVN) was 3.0% higher at 97.80 soles ($30.74), while financial holding company  \uc2 Credicorp Ltd.\uc2  (BAP) gained 1.3% to end at $49.80.   \par \par \uc2 Zinc miner Compania Minera Volcan SAA (VOLB.VL) rose 6.2% to end at PEN10.19 in heavy trade.   \par \par \uc2 The other sector to fare well Tuesday was the telecommunications sector.   \par \par \uc2 Telefonica SA\uc2 's (TEF) Telefonica del Peru (TELEFBC1.VL), for example, rose 6.5% to PEN2.79.   \par \par \uc2 "Investors are turning to companies like Telefonica right now as they are anticipating good first-quarter company results and dividends," the trader said.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the sol ended stronger Tuesday at a midrate of PEN3.1810 to the U.S. dollar on a spread of PEN3.1800 to PEN3.1820.   \par \par \uc2 The sol had ended the previous session at a midrate of PEN3.1835 to the U.S. dollar on a spread of PEN3.1830 to PEN3.1840.   \par \par \uc2 A market analyst said the sol strengthened due to demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from mining companies to make tax payments.   \par \par \uc2 The Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to purchase $100 million.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in recent sessions to buy dollars to ease movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and to build foreign reserves.   \par \par \uc2 -By Rebecca Howard and Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires;peru@dowjones.com; 51-1-221 7050 [ 04-03-07 1628ET ]   \par \par \uc2 1071 1837   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070403e34300074\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
