\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Main Stock Index Rises 2.14% To End At Record   \b0\par\par\uc2 467 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2007\par 22:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's main stock indexes climbed to new record highs Monday, marking the eighth consecutive session of gains, as expectations of strong first-quarter earnings lured investors.   \par \par \uc2 According to preliminary results from the Lima Stock Exchange, the stock market's broad general index rose 367.81 points, or 2.14%, to end at 17,520.63.   \par \par \uc2 The selective blue-chip index increased 731.58 points, or 2.55%, to finish at 29,444.06.   \par \par \uc2 "The market is solid here. Companies just wrapped up the quarter and analysts and investors are expecting good results and good dividends. That is what is what is leading to a buying wave right now," one trader said.   \par \par \uc2 He also pointed out that several mining companies aren't only benefiting from stronger metals prices but also from expansions.   \par \par \uc2 In particular, he pointed to copper miner Sociedad Minera Cerro Verde SAA (CVERDEC1.VL) and base metals miner  \uc2 Compania Minera Milpo\uc2  SAA (MILPOC1).   \par \par \uc2 Cerro Verde gained 7.5% to $21.29 on Monday, while Milpo gained 5% to 14.60 soles. ($4.59)   \par \par \uc2 Cerro Verde recently completed an $850 million expansion that will strongly increase its output this year. The company has said it expects to produce 250,000 metric tons of copper this year.   \par \par \uc2 Milpo expects its Cerro Lindo polymetallic project to come onstream in May.   \par \par \uc2 Other miners continued their steady climb, with  \uc2 Southern Copper Corp.\uc2  (PCU) up 1.2% to end at $72.50, and  \uc2 Compania de Minas Buenaventura\uc2  SAA (BVN) up 0.5% at PEN95.00.   \par \par \uc2 Financial holding company  \uc2 Credicorp Ltd.\uc2  (BAP) gained 1.1% to end at $49.16.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the sol ended slightly weaker Monday at a midrate of PEN3.1835 to the U.S. dollar on a spread of PEN3.1830 to PEN3.1840.   \par \par \uc2 The sol had ended Friday at a midrate of PEN3.1825 to the U.S. dollar on a spread of PEN3.1820 to PEN3.1830.   \par \par \uc2 The Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to purchase $65 million.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly in recent sessions to buy dollars to ease movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and to build foreign reserves.   \par \par \uc2 Scotiabank noted in a report that in the first quarter the sol appreciated by 0.4% against the U.S. dollar, and had appreciated some 2.6% compared with the average exchange rate in 2006.   \par \par \uc2 It added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had accumulated some $1.53 billion so far this year, some 36% of the purchases it made in all of 2006. That didn't include Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 -By Rebecca Howard and Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires;\b \highlight22\uc2 peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 @dowjones.com; 51-1-221 7050 [ 04-02-07 1629ET ]   \par \par \uc2 1071 1837   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020070402e3420008k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
