\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Economy Hits Sweet Spot, Growth Estimates Upped   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Kozak   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,055 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2006\par 17:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Peru's economy, which has expanded for five straight years, has hit a sweet spot, with inflation low and gross domestic product forecast to grow even more strongly in the short-term.   \par \par \uc2 Government revenues are robust, while the local stock market is hitting record highs as investors pour cash into local companies.   \par \par \uc2 A boom in mineral prices has propelled exports to record levels, while confident consumers are opening their wallets to buy cars and other consumer goods.   \par \par \uc2 "If the external conditions remain favorable, and with our policies of growth in public investments and promotion of economic competitiveness, this nation should be growing between 6.0% and 7.0% in GDP in the coming years," Finance Minister Luiz Carranza said in a meeting with the foreign press this week.   \par \par \uc2 While economists were earlier forecasting growth of around 5.0% this year, many of those earlier forecasts are now being revised, especially following June's 6.9% gain in GDP. The economy expanded some 6.5% in 2005.   \par \par \uc2 Banco BBVA Continental recently bumped up its GDP forecast to 6.2% for this year, while consultants Macroconsult now forecast growth of 6.9% for 2006.   \par \par \uc2 Bear Stearns said in a report that previous 5.5% to 6.0% growth forecasts may be too conservative. "Peru continues to benefit from tremendous growth momentum, something that has taken market confidence in the performance of the economy to three-year highs," it said.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the government of President Alan Garcia, which took office in July, has promised to sharply increase public sector spending, in what it calls an "investment shock," while keeping the fiscal deficit modest.   \par \par \uc2 Despite the vigorous growth, consumer prices remain under control. The 12-month inflation rate to the end of August was 1.87%, at the lower end of the target range set out by the Central Reserve Bank of Peru.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Raw Materials Boost Exports \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite political volatility, successive governments since the early 1990s have promoted, with some exceptions, free market policies that have cheered investors. Those policies alongside a boom in raw material prices have created growing confidence in the economy.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sales of gold, copper, zinc, silver and other minerals have led the export gains. But exports of other products such as fish meal, coffee and textiles have also taken off.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overall exports rose to a record $2.3 billion in July, 48.3% higher than in the same month a year earlier, and the highest monthly total on record.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To take advantage of the strong demand, various mining companies are expanding, such as Sociedad Minera Cerro Verde SAA's (CVERDEC1.VL) $850 million upgrade at its copper mine in southern Peru. Copper miner Southern Copper Corp.(PCU) has a large scale modernization underway.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer product companies are also pouring cash into the nation, with beverage maker Union de Cervecerias Peruanas Backus & Johnston SAA, controlled by SABMiller PLC, (SAB.JO) recently announcing a $102 million investment. Canada's Bank of Nova Scotia (BNS) recently purchased Peru's third-biggest bank, while HSBC Holdings PLC (HBC) has started the regulatory process to be able to open offices in the Andean nation.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumers and others have also taken heart, a number of indicators show.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Domestic demand jumped 8.6% in the first six months of this year over the same period a year before. New vehicle sales increased 41% in the first six months of the year.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consultants Apoyo Consultoria's index of consumer confidence rose in June to a record high as Peruvians start to sense that the robust economic activity is finally trickling down to benefit them.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Political Calm Helps \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say a return to political calm following the elections earlier this year has helped sway investors.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Radical nationalist Ollanta Humala, who at one point led in opinion polls, failed to win the presidency, and so far President Alan Garcia's policies have been welcomed by markets.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Lima Stock Exchange has posted some of the strongest gains in the world this year, with the broad general index hitting a new record high Thursday. To the end of August, the bolsa's general index has increased 108%, driven in good part by mining company shares.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the sol, has appreciated 5.1% against the U.S. dollar this year, according to Banco Wiese Sudameris.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Central Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Peru\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has had to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy record amounts of dollars to keep the sol from strengthening too much, purchasing $1.41 billion in August alone.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Peru's sovereign bonds have also rallied. The country's risk premium on JPMorgan's Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus closed Thursday at 135 basis points over Treasurys, close to the record low of 128 basis points.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    To Post External Surplus \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The burgeoning economy has also helped lift tax revenues, which posted a real 56% increase in July over