\par\par \b \uc2 IMF Approves Review Of Peru's Standby Loan  \b0\par\par\uc2 447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2006\par 17:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--The International Monetary Fund on Wednesday said it had approved the third review of Peru's 26-month Standby loan program, first granted in June 2004.  \par \par \uc2 The approval makes available some $296 million, although Peru hasn't and doesn't plan to draw on any of the funds. The overall pact was worth about $422.8 million when it was first signed.  \par \par \uc2 In a letter of intent sent to the IMF Dec. 20 and published Wednesday on the Finance Ministry's Web site, the Peruvian government says "fiscal discipline will continue as the center of a strategy to consolidate macroeconomic stability."  \par \par \uc2 In the letter, the government says Peru's gross domestic product will grow around 5% in real terms in 2006 and inflation will be 2.5%.  \par \par \uc2 In 2005, the GDP expanded by 6.3% according to President Alejandro Toledo. The consumer price index rose 1.49% in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 The Central Reserve Bank of Peru aims to keep consumer inflation within a one percentage point range - with 2.5% serving as the midpoint - into the indefinite future.  \par \par \uc2 The letter of intent forecasts a fiscal deficit of 1.0% of GDP for 2006.  \par \par \uc2 In 2005 the fiscal deficit was 0.6% of GDP, according to Finance Minister Fernando Zavala.  \par \par \uc2 Among other points, the letter said the government will continue to focus on tax reform, seeking to eliminate regional tax exemptions.  \par \par \uc2 It will also concentrate on reducing tax evasion, it said.  \par \par \uc2 Peru's tax revenues, excluding certain contributions, reached 28.04 billion soles ($8.15 billion) in 2005, a 14.7% increase in real terms over the previous year.  \par \par \uc2 Regarding the exchange rate, the letter emphasized that the Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  will only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to control excessive volatility.  \par \par \uc2 In recent sessions the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling dollars in order to shore up the sol as financial markets are affected by political concerns in the run-up to April's general elections.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, efforts to de-dollarize the economy will continue.  \par \par \uc2 For example, 50% of bank loans under a government sponsored housing program in the January - June 2006 period must be granted in the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the government said in its letter of intent.  \par \par \uc2 Peruvian officials have said they want to discuss an exit strategy from negotiating standby arrangements with the IMF once the current deal ends, arguing that the economy is strong and the fiscal situation is in order.  \par \par \uc2 The current agreement will expire shortly after a new government takes office July 28.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By Rebecca Howard, Dow Jones Newswires; 511-221-7050; peru@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-01-06 1601GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060111e21b000o0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
