\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's Sol  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  May Suffer Volatile Ride Through 2006  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Robert Kozak  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 879 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Januar 2006\par 20:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LIMA (Dow Jones)--Uncertainty tied to April's general elections could rattle Peru's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first half of 2006, but strong economic fundamentals may help calm the sol later in the year.  \par \par \uc2 The rise of nationalist candidate Ollanta Humala in opinion polls in the later part of 2005 caused volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, leading to ongoing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm investors.  \par \par \uc2 That unrest may continue, economists say.  \par \par \uc2 "The most probable is that speculation will predominate in the first half the year and then in the second half the fundamentals will be more important," said Banco Wiese Sudameris' chief economist Guillermo Diaz.  \par \par \uc2 Various elections scheduled in other Latin American nations this year could also create volatility in the Peruvian market.  \par \par \uc2 Banco Wiese Sudameris forecasts that the sol could average PEN3.42 per dollar in 2006, a depreciation of about 3.7% compared with the 2005 average.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                       Sol Stability Overturned \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sol has been relatively stable for the past five or so years, floating between PEN3.25 per dollar and PEN3.62 per dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened by some 4.3% in 2005 against the dollar, ending the last trading session of the year at a midrate of PEN3.4185 per dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That depreciation came about despite Peru's robust exports, tame inflation, and fiscal position well under control.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  actually strengthened in the first part of 2005, reaching an average interbank rate of PEN3.252 per dollar in July.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Record high prices for minerals have led to a boom in Peruvian exports, particularly in the mining sector, bolstering the sol. Remittances by Peruvians living abroad increased cash flows into the Andean nation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regularly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy dollars in the first part of the year, helping build the international reserves and ease the appreciation of the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials say that dollar buying by private pensions funds in Peru in the mid-year started the trend toward a weakening of the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rising short-term interest rates in the U.S. also helped damp interest in Peruvian investments, while Peru's key reference rate, set by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , remained steady.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sol also began to weaken alongside poll results that showed presidential hopeful Humala gaining popularity before next April's election for president.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the sol weakened, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  responded by selling U.S. dollars in October for the first time in about three years. It also began placing certificates of deposit indexed to the exchange rate, which function the same as dollar sales.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the last month of the year, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also raised its key reference rate by 25 basis points to 3.25%, citing in part \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market volatility.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    Downplay Volatility \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government officials downplay the ability of speculative efforts to seriously weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before the elections, saying the international reserves are much more than the entire monetary base in soles. The U.S. dollar is also legal tender in Peru and is used for many larger purchases such as real estate and vehicles.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central Reserve Bank acting President Oscar Dancourt said in a recent radio interview that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has prepared for any unrest by building the international reserves over the last few years, purchasing a net $998 million in 2003, $1.85 billion in 2004 and $767 million in 2005.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Peru's net international reserves totaled $14.34 billion as of Dec. 28, while the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  position, or the cash available to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , totaled $7.52 billion as of Dec. 28.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We have important dollar reserves and can meet any increased demand for dollars caused by the election process, without putting at risk the maintenance of macroeconomic successes," Dancourt said, adding that the exchange rate is one of the toughest variables to forecast.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has remained active in easing volatility in the market so far in 2006, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell U.S. dollars on both Tuesday and Wednesday this week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                    Fundamentals Remain Solid \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists continue to point to Peru's solid economic fundamentals as a positive factor, with gross domestic product expected to expand some 5.0% in 2006.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although the economy is expected to have expanded by some 6.0% in 2005, inflation has dwindled, with consumer prices rising by only 1.49% in 2005.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The fundamentals point out that in 2006 (setting aside the political theme) the Peruvian economy will maintain an important rhythm of growth, with low inflation and a fiscal deficit of around 1.0% of GDP and an important balance in the trade balance that will allow the current account in the balance of payments to remain in surplus," Banco de Credito said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That bank expects the sol to weaken by some 1.9% in 2006 against the U.S. dollar. "The depreciation of the sol is expected to be moderate," it said in a recent report.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (This is the fourth in a series of six stories on the near-term outlooks for major Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Robert Kozak, Dow Jones Newswires;511-221-7050;peru@dowjones.com [ 05-01-06 1940GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060105e215000s3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
